# Your Complete Guide to Full-Stack Development

Full-stack development is one of the most sought-after skills in tech. This comprehensive guide will introduce you to the fundamental concepts and help you understand how different pieces fit together to create complete web applications.

## What is Full-Stack Development?

A full-stack developer is someone who can work on both the **frontend** (what users see and interact with) and the **backend** (server, database, and application logic). Think of it as being able to build the entire house, not just the facade or the plumbing.

### The Full Stack Consists Of:

1. **Frontend** - HTML, CSS, JavaScript (React, Vue, Angular)
2. **Backend** - Server logic (Node.js, Python, Java, Go)
3. **Database** - Data storage (PostgreSQL, MongoDB, MySQL)
4. **DevOps** - Deployment and infrastructure (Docker, AWS, Vercel)

## How Web Applications Work

Understanding the request-response cycle is fundamental:

```
┌─────────────┐      HTTP Request       ┌─────────────┐
│   Browser   │ ───────────────────────> │   Server    │
│  (Frontend) │                          │  (Backend)  │
│             │ <─────────────────────── │             │
└─────────────┘      HTTP Response       └─────────────┘
                                                ↓
                                         ┌─────────────┐
                                         │  Database   │
                                         └─────────────┘
```

### Example Flow:
1. User clicks "Sign Up" button (Frontend)
2. Browser sends POST request to `/api/signup` (HTTP)
3. Server receives request, validates data (Backend)
4. Server saves user to database (Database)
5. Server sends response back to browser
6. Frontend shows success message

## HTTP Basics Every Full-Stack Developer Needs

### HTTP Methods

```javascript
// GET - Retrieve data
GET /api/users           // Get all users
GET /api/users/123       // Get specific user

// POST - Create new data
POST /api/users
{ "name": "John", "email": "john@example.com" }

// PUT - Update entire resource
PUT /api/users/123
{ "name": "John Doe", "email": "john@example.com" }

// PATCH - Update partial resource
PATCH /api/users/123
{ "name": "John Doe" }

// DELETE - Remove data
DELETE /api/users/123
```

### Status Codes

- **200 OK** - Request succeeded
- **201 Created** - New resource created
- **400 Bad Request** - Invalid data sent
- **401 Unauthorized** - Authentication required
- **404 Not Found** - Resource doesn't exist
- **500 Server Error** - Something went wrong on server

## Building Your First Full-Stack App

Let's build a simple todo application to understand the concepts.

### Frontend (React)

```javascript
import { useState, useEffect } from 'react';

function TodoApp() {
  const [todos, setTodos] = useState([]);
  const [newTodo, setNewTodo] = useState('');

  // Fetch todos from backend
  useEffect(() => {
    fetch('http://localhost:3001/api/todos')
      .then(res => res.json())
      .then(data => setTodos(data));
  }, []);

  // Create new todo
  const handleSubmit = async (e) => {
    e.preventDefault();

    const response = await fetch('http://localhost:3001/api/todos', {
      method: 'POST',
      headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
      body: JSON.stringify({ title: newTodo, completed: false })
    });

    const todo = await response.json();
    setTodos([...todos, todo]);
    setNewTodo('');
  };

  // Delete todo
  const handleDelete = async (id) => {
    await fetch(`http://localhost:3001/api/todos/${id}`, {
      method: 'DELETE'
    });

    setTodos(todos.filter(todo => todo.id !== id));
  };

  return (
    <div>
      <h1>My Todos</h1>

      <form onSubmit={handleSubmit}>
        <input
          value={newTodo}
          onChange={(e) => setNewTodo(e.target.value)}
          placeholder="Add new todo"
        />
        <button type="submit">Add</button>
      </form>

      <ul>
        {todos.map(todo => (
          <li key={todo.id}>
            {todo.title}
            <button onClick={() => handleDelete(todo.id)}>Delete</button>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </div>
  );
}

export default TodoApp;
```

### Backend (Node.js + Express)

```javascript
const express = require('express');
const cors = require('cors');
const app = express();

app.use(cors());
app.use(express.json());

// In-memory storage (in real apps, use a database)
let todos = [
  { id: 1, title: 'Learn HTML', completed: true },
  { id: 2, title: 'Learn JavaScript', completed: false }
];

let nextId = 3;

// GET all todos
app.get('/api/todos', (req, res) => {
  res.json(todos);
});

// GET single todo
app.get('/api/todos/:id', (req, res) => {
  const todo = todos.find(t => t.id === parseInt(req.params.id));

  if (!todo) {
    return res.status(404).json({ error: 'Todo not found' });
  }

  res.json(todo);
});

// POST create new todo
app.post('/api/todos', (req, res) => {
  const { title, completed } = req.body;

  if (!title) {
    return res.status(400).json({ error: 'Title is required' });
  }

  const newTodo = {
    id: nextId++,
    title,
    completed: completed || false
  };

  todos.push(newTodo);
  res.status(201).json(newTodo);
});

// PUT update todo
app.put('/api/todos/:id', (req, res) => {
  const id = parseInt(req.params.id);
  const index = todos.findIndex(t => t.id === id);

  if (index === -1) {
    return res.status(404).json({ error: 'Todo not found' });
  }

  todos[index] = { ...todos[index], ...req.body };
  res.json(todos[index]);
});

// DELETE todo
app.delete('/api/todos/:id', (req, res) => {
  const id = parseInt(req.params.id);
  todos = todos.filter(t => t.id !== id);
  res.status(204).send();
});

const PORT = 3001;
app.listen(PORT, () => {
  console.log(`Server running on http://localhost:${PORT}`);
});
```

